/**
 * @mainpage truss — build your C++ using C++
 *
 * <center><b>Build your C++ using C++.</b> A small, fast build system whose
 * build scripts are ordinary C++26 programs: a mate.h-style fluent API,
 * xmake-class features, first-class C++20/26 modules, zig-powered
 * cross-compilation, and a samurai (ninja) backend — no configure step, no
 * DSL, no config file.</center>
 *
 * @tableofcontents
 *
 * @section main_intro What truss is
 *
 * A truss project has no build-system files. Its build script is a C++26
 * program (`truss.cpp`) that describes targets with a fluent, type-safe API
 * and calls `Project::build()`. That program is compiled once into a
 * `./truss` binary which then builds, tests and runs the project. The script
 * re-runs (cheaply) on every invocation, regenerating a ninja manifest that
 * the vendored samurai executes incrementally; when the script itself
 * changes, truss recompiles and re-execs itself (see @ref main_selfhost).
 *
 * Highlights:
 *
 * - **C++ modules that just work** — automatic BMI wiring with the full
 *   transitive closure across `depends_on`, plus real `import std;` on
 *   clang + libc++ (see @ref main_modules).
 * - **Cross-compile with one line** — `Toolchain::Zig` + `target("...")`
 *   reaches musl, glibc-pinned, aarch64 and Windows-gnu targets with bundled
 *   sysroots (see @ref main_cross).
 * - **Static & portable binaries** — per-dependency `static_link()`,
 *   `static_deps()` and `static_stdlib()` (fully-static musl, or drop just
 *   the C++ runtime) (see @ref main_static).
 * - **Packages** — `fetch` (git/tarball), `conan` and `vcpkg`, consumed as
 *   targets (see @ref main_deps).
 * - **Fast & incremental** — compiler-cache launcher, unity builds, PCH,
 *   precise module wiring.
 * - **Hardened by default** — stack protector, RELRO, non-exec stack and
 *   libc++ hardening tiers, per target (see @ref main_options).
 *
 * @section main_quickstart Quick start
 *
 * @code
 * // truss.cpp
 * import truss;
 *
 * int main(int argc, char** argv) {
 *     truss::Project proj("Engine", argc, argv);
 *
 *     auto& core = proj.add_static_library("core")
 *                      .include_dirs("include")
 *                      .sources_in("src/core");
 *
 *     proj.add_executable("app")
 *         .sources_in("src/app")
 *         .depends_on(core);
 *
 *     proj.add_executable("test_core")
 *         .sources_in("tests")
 *         .depends_on(core)
 *         .is_test();
 *
 *     return proj.build() ? 0 : 1;
 * }
 * @endcode
 *
 * @code
 * ./truss debug          # build into build/debug/
 * ./truss test           # build + run every is_test() target
 * ./truss run            # build + run the first executable
 * ./truss release test   # release mode + tests
 * ./truss -n             # dry run: emit the graph, run nothing
 * ./truss --target app   # build one target only
 * @endcode
 *
 * @section main_get Getting truss
 *
 * truss is a source library. Clone it and bootstrap once:
 *
 * @code
 * ./setup.sh build              # libtruss.a + BMIs + the self-host ./truss
 * ./setup.sh install <prefix>   # optional: stage libtruss.a + BMIs + truss CLI
 * ./setup.sh test               # unit + examples + installed + amalgam
 * @endcode
 *
 * A new project then contains just `truss.cpp` (+ a `bootstrap.sh` linking
 * the script against the staged module); `truss init <name>` scaffolds one.
 * The unit tests run through `./setup.sh unit` (doctest, ASan/UBSan).
 *
 * @section main_targets Targets
 *
 * Five target kinds cover the build graph:
 *
 * | Creator | Artifact | Notes |
 * |---|---|---|
 * | \ref truss::Project::add_executable "add_executable" | linked binary | also the `run` default |
 * | \ref truss::Project::add_static_library "add_static_library" | `lib<name>.a` | the common case |
 * | \ref truss::Project::add_shared_library "add_shared_library" | `lib<name>.so`/`.dll` | soname + rpath wiring |
 * | \ref truss::Project::add_interface_library "add_interface_library" | none | usage requirements only (CMake INTERFACE) |
 * | \ref truss::Project::import_library "import_library" | none — prebuilt | files linked verbatim + rpath |
 * | \ref truss::Project::add_custom_command "add_custom_command" | generated files | the codegen pattern |
 *
 * Every builder method is a deducing-`this` template returning the target's
 * own type (`StaticLib&`, `Executable&`, ...), so chains keep the derived
 * type and derived-only methods can follow base ones in any order.
 *
 * Sources are glob patterns (`src/**.cpp`, `?`, `{a,b}` alternation; hidden
 * entries skipped, results sorted) or literal paths. Usage requirements
 * propagate to dependents CMake-INTERFACE-style: include dirs always,
 * defines/link flags from `Interface` targets only — user libraries never
 * leak private defines into consumers.
 *
 * @section main_options Options, hardening, PCH, unity
 *
 * Per target: `cpp_standard()`, `warnings()`, `debug_info()`,
 * `optimization()`, `sanitizer()`, `lto()`, `harden()` (Light by default:
 * stack protector + RELRO + non-exec stack; Medium/Max add pattern-filled
 * locals, CFI, stack-clash probing, libc++ hardening modes), `pch()`
 * (clang `-include-pch`, GCC `.gch` with the PCH dir searched first),
 * `unity()` (batch TUs of 16 sources, module units never batched) and the
 * raw `flags()` / `linker_flags()` escape hatches. Full details in the
 * \ref grp_api "API reference" and the README's "Compiler options" section.
 *
 * @section main_modules C++20/26 modules
 *
 * `.cppm` sources are first-class: truss scans them (regex heuristic by
 * default; clang-scan-deps in `ModulesMode::Manual`), emits one BMI edge per
 * interface and wires every consumer with the full transitive
 * `-fmodule-file=` closure across `depends_on` — including partitions and
 * `import std;` / `import std.compat;` on clang + libc++. Module units are
 * never unity-batched; zig targets and MSVC refuse module builds with clear
 * Config errors. See the README's "C++ modules" section.
 *
 * @section main_static Static linking
 *
 * `static_link()` builds a shared library as an archive instead;
 * `static_deps()` routes every dependency of an executable/shared library
 * through `-Wl,-Bstatic` (shared libs from a static twin archive of the same
 * PIC objects); `static_stdlib()` drops the C++ runtime dependency (Gcc/Llvm/
 * Musl flavors, validated against the toolchain). Archives are ordered
 * consumers-before-providers on every link line, so diamond dependency
 * graphs resolve with a single left-to-right ld pass.
 *
 * @section main_deps Dependencies
 *
 * - \ref truss::Project::add_subdirectory "add_subdirectory" — in-tree static
 *   library by convention (its `src/` + `include/`).
 * - \ref truss::Project::fetch "fetch" — git (tag/branch/commit) or tarball
 *   (sha256-verified, cached in `~/.cache/truss`, pinned in `truss.lock`
 *   under an advisory file lock).
 * - \ref truss::Project::conan "conan" / \ref truss::Project::vcpkg "vcpkg" —
 *   package-manager graphs consumed as one `Interface` target.
 * - \ref truss::Project::import_library "import_library" — prebuilt archives
 *   you already have.
 * - \ref truss::Project::add_custom_command "add_custom_command" — the
 *   codegen pattern: a `for_host()` tool feeds generated sources into the
 *   build, with `$tool`/`$out`/`$in` placeholders and dependency tracking.
 * - `task()` / `pre_build()` / `post_build()` — custom tasks and hooks.
 *
 * @section main_cross Cross-compiling
 *
 * `proj.configure(truss::Config{}.toolchain(truss::Toolchain::Zig).target("aarch64-linux-musl"))`
 * cross-compiles the whole project; host-side tools are pinned with
 * `for_host()`, and a `depends_on()` edge may not cross the host/target
 * boundary (different ABI/C library — a Config error). Per-target
 * `cross_target()` overrides the project setting. For several triples at
 * once, `proj.targets({{"linux", Config{}}, {"win", ...}})` builds one
 * subgraph per configuration in a single invocation. Windows targets come
 * via mingw-w64 (GCC, target-prefixed driver) or llvm-mingw (clang: modules
 * + `import std` cross); outputs run under wine. See the
 * \ref cross_compilation_page "cross-compilation strategy" page for the
 * triple conventions, runners and known gaps.
 *
 * @section main_scripting Scripting helpers
 *
 * Build scripts also get \ref truss::os "os" (run/capture, env, memoized
 * `find_program`, `temp_scope`, machine specs), \ref truss::fs "fs"
 * (globbing, the generator-based walk/read_lines, text I/O, `find_library`)
 * and \ref truss::log "log" (leveled, colored output) — everything a build
 * script otherwise shells out for, all `Result`-based.
 *
 * @section main_errors Errors
 *
 * Everything fallible returns `truss::Result<T>` (`std::expected<T, Error>`);
 * no exceptions cross the API. `Error` carries a kind, a rich message (with
 * correct/incorrect examples where that helps) and an optional path. A failed
 * `Result<TargetRef>` handed to `depends_on()`/`uses()`/`merge()` is kept and
 * reported by `Project::build()` — never dropped.
 *
 * @section main_selfhost Building truss itself
 *
 * truss builds itself: `./truss.cpp` (repo root) is a truss build script —
 * `./setup.sh build` bootstraps `libtruss.a` + BMIs and compiles `./truss`,
 * which then rebuilds truss with truss (`./truss debug`), including a
 * self-rebuild + re-exec when the script or the module sources change.
 *
 * @section main_docs Where to read next
 *
 * - \ref grp_api "Public API reference" — Project, Target, Config,
 *   Error/Result, the os/fs/log namespaces: every builder, every flag, every
 *   task.
 * - \ref internals_page "Internals" — the module structure, the build flow
 *   (validate → resolve → emit → execute), the subsystem file map and the
 *   invariants.
 * - \ref grp_internals "Internals group" — the internal declarations of the
 *   `truss:impl` partition (invisible to `import truss;`).
 * - \ref cross_compilation_page "Cross-compilation strategy" — triple
 *   conventions, runners, the host/target boundary rules, known gaps.
 * - \ref roadmap_page "1.0 roadmap" — where the project is heading.
 * - In the repository: README.MD (user guide), PLAN.MD (the original plan),
 *   AGENT.MD (constraints & verified toolchain facts), examples/ (working
 *   scripts for every feature: modules, cross-host, fetch, conan, vcpkg,
 *   imported, shared, amalgam, installed, hello).
 */
